---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GovernAuthorizeVerify from "../components/diagrams/GovernAuthorizeVerify.astro";
import ReleaseLifecycle from "../components/diagrams/ReleaseLifecycle.astro";
import VerificationHierarchy from "../components/diagrams/VerificationHierarchy.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Platform | BeaconGuard Govern, Authorize, Verify"
  description="BeaconGuard provides Govern, Authorize, and Verify for regulated AI actions: Active Verified Releases, Request Admission Authorization, Result-to-Action Authorization, and read-only verification."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Platform capabilities for governed AI transactions</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard is structured as three functional domains: Govern, Authorize,
      and Verify. It governs whether an enterprise application may invoke an AI
      provider and whether a declared enterprise action based on an AI-generated
      result may proceed. AI output remains an untrusted proposal. Provider
      success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Govern · Authorize · Verify</h2>
    <p>
      These are functional domains, not assumed network planes. Govern builds
      and activates the control set. Authorize returns binary ALLOW or DENY at
      request admission and at the declared intended action. Verify investigates
      decision evidence without granting runtime authority.
    </p>
    <GovernAuthorizeVerify />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Existing systems remain in place</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard does not replace the system of record, workflow application,
      case-management platform, EHR, AML system, fraud system, SIEM, IAM, or GRC
      tool. Those systems continue to own their existing functions. BeaconGuard
      authorizes; the enterprise application executes; the enterprise system of
      record remains authoritative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Govern</h2>
    <p>
      Govern covers environment and integration configuration, Integration
      Policy, optional System Connector mapping, runtime topology and trust,
      Compliance Playbooks, validation, approval, signing, distribution, and
      activation of an Active Verified Release.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Governed release lifecycle</h2>
    <p>
      Compliance Playbooks are validated, compiled, approved, signed, and
      distributed as immutable release artifacts. Distribution alone does not
      activate a release. Release Verification and Activation must succeed
      before the artifact becomes the Active Verified Release used for
      authorization.
    </p>
    <ReleaseLifecycle />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Compliance Playbooks</h2>
    <p>
      Compliance Playbooks express governed control intent for regulated
      workflows. They are governed artifacts packaged into releases—not legal
      advice and not a guarantee of compliance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Authorize</h2>
    <p>
      Authorize evaluates governed requests against the Active Verified Release
      and returns <strong>ALLOW</strong> or <strong>DENY</strong> only. Review
      may produce obligations or grants for a later request; it does not convert
      a recorded DENY into ALLOW and does not resume an original provider
      response as authority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Request Admission Authorization</h2>
    <p>
      Determines whether an enterprise application may invoke the AI provider
      under the Active Verified Release.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>DENY records the decision and blocks provider invocation.</li>
      <li>ALLOW permits provider invocation for that governed request.</li>
      <li>
        Where enforcement requires it, evidence is recorded before upstream
        forward.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Result-to-Action Authorization</h2>
    <p>
      After an AI-generated result returns, the enterprise application declares
      the intended enterprise action. BeaconGuard authorizes or denies that
      <em>declared</em> action under the Active Verified Release. This is
      independent authorization of the intended enterprise action—not semantic
      scoring of model-response content.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The AI-generated result remains an untrusted proposal.</li>
      <li>DENY prevents enterprise effect for the declared action.</li>
      <li>
        ALLOW authorizes execution by the enterprise application; BeaconGuard
        does not become the system of record.
      </li>
      <li>
        Approving a proposal does not resume the original AI response as
        authority.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>System Connector</h2>
    <p>
      An optional System Connector maps source protocols and context into the
      required request format when enterprise applications do not natively
      produce that structure. The System Connector does not evaluate controls,
      activate releases, or bypass fail-closed enforcement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Verify</h2>
    <p>
      Authorization decisions produce Verifiable Decision Records that support
      investigation through BeaconGuard Verify. Verify is a read-only surface
      for reporting, exploration, reconstruction, source evidence, replay
      verification, and verifiable export.
    </p>
    <VerificationHierarchy />
    <p>
      BeaconGuard Verify is read-only. It cannot modify evidence, change a
      historical decision, activate a release, alter a Compliance Playbook,
      grant runtime authority, or authorize an enterprise action.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/verification">Explore Verification</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/proof-evidence">Inspect Proof and Evidence</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Enterprise Identity and Privileged Access</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard integrates with enterprise identity systems while preserving
      the customer identity provider as the workforce identity authority.
      Governed management operations can use federated identity, lifecycle
      provisioning, role and tenant boundaries, privileged step-up, session
      revocation, and controlled administrative access.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>OIDC and SAML workforce federation</li>
      <li>SCIM lifecycle integration where configured</li>
      <li>Workload and service identities</li>
      <li>Tenant-scoped and delegated administration</li>
      <li>Privileged step-up for sensitive operations</li>
      <li>Session revocation and offboarding controls</li>
      <li>Controlled break-glass and support-access boundaries</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard does not replace IAM, the customer directory, or the customer
      identity provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Evidence ordering and integrity</h2>
    <p>
      Unauthorized actions are hard-stopped. Decisions produce evidence for
      investigation. Where enforcement requires it, evidence is recorded before
      forward. Retention, durability, and integrity protection depend on the
      configured evidence provider and customer deployment. Legal admissibility
      is not claimed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Industry capability surfaces</h2>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--compact">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Healthcare</h3>
        <p>
          Governed authorization for PHI-sensitive operational AI workflows.
          BeaconGuard does not certify clinical correctness.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/healthcare">Review Healthcare</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Financial</h3>
        <p>
          Request admission and action authorization for AML, KYC, fraud,
          dispute, and recommendation workflows.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/fintech">Review Financial</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Regulated automation</h3>
        <p>
          Fail-closed authorization before agents or automations produce
          enterprise effect.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/canonical-workflow">Review Workflow Examples</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deep technical paths</h2>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/how-it-works">See How It Works</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/verification">Verification</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/docs/architecture">System Architecture</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/docs/enforcement-runtime">Enforcement Runtime</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/healthcare">Healthcare path</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/fintech">Financial path</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
